<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganges–Yamuna Doab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ganga-Yamuna Doab, Ganga–Yamuna Doab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> Doab is the fertile alluvial land between the Ganges and Yamuna rivers in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, stretching from the Shivalik foothills to their confluence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a>. One of the most productive farming regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, it was the heartland of the late Vedic Kuru and Panchala realms and later a core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. Its name comes from the Persian for two waters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganges-yamuna-doab.jpg" alt="Ganges–Yamuna Doab" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fowler&amp;fowler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punjabdoabs1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Pratihara empire drew its wealth from the agriculture of the <b>Ganges–Yamuna Doab</b> and the plains of Malwa and Rajasthan, where wheat, barley, rice, sugar cane and cotton were grown.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It did not expand east of the <b>Ganga-Yamuna Doab</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/painted-grey-ware-culture">Painted Grey Ware culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After about 1000 BCE iron axes and ploughs made it possible to clear the forests of the Gangetic plains; Vedic settlements spread into the western <b>Ganga-Yamuna Doab</b>, growing wheat, rice and barley, and new crafts arose, among them carpentry, tanning, pottery, jewellery, dyeing and winemaking.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad Khan Bangash, an Afghan commander who served the Mughal emperor Farrukhsiyar, was granted lands in the <b>Doab</b> and founded the town of Farrukhabad in 1714, naming it after the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangash-nawabs-of-farrukhabad">Bangash Nawabs of Farrukhabad</a></li><li><blockquote>Chandradeva seized Kannauj around 1090 and also controlled Varanasi, Ayodhya and the <b>Doab</b>, founding a line that soon dominated the middle Ganges plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultans held Awadh and much of the <b>Doab</b> and frequently challenged the Delhi Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaunpur-sultanate">Jaunpur Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
